INT. RED CANYON TRADING POST - AFTERNOON

A long adobe room with a plank counter and shelves of flour, tobacco, and tin cups. Shafts of afternoon light cross the dust. A stuffed buffalo head looks down from the back wall, one glass eye missing. A stove burns low.

Behind the counter stands EZEKIEL TALLCHIEF-ROURKE, sixty, the proprietor, gray-bearded, with sleeves rolled to the elbow. At a corner table sit two cowhands, WILEY BARNES, thirties, long and sunburned, playing solitaire, and JOSIAH COLTRANE, twenties, round-faced and quiet, whittling. A fourth man, REV. ABEL MOREHOUSE, fifties, sits by the window in a dusty black coat, reading from a small Bible without looking at the words.

A clock ticks. The whittling stops.

EZEKIEL
Coffee's on if you want it.

WILEY
I'd take a cup.

EZEKIEL
Josiah?

JOSIAH
No, thank you, sir.

EZEKIEL
Reverend?

REV. MOREHOUSE
I'm well, Mr. Rourke.

Ezekiel pours Wiley a cup and sets it on the table. Wiley turns a card, grunts.

WILEY
Hot enough to scald a snake.

EZEKIEL
That's how you know it's coffee.

JOSIAH
My ma used to say a man who drinks cold coffee has nothing to hurry for.

EZEKIEL
Your ma sounds like a sensible woman.

JOSIAH
She was, sir.

Wiley glances at Josiah, then at the reverend, then down at his cards. He lays down another. The clock ticks louder.

WILEY
Reverend, you've been on that same page for twenty minutes.

REV. MOREHOUSE
It's a long verse.

WILEY
It's one line.

REV. MOREHOUSE
Then it has a lot to say.

Ezekiel wipes the counter with a gray rag, slow, circular. Outside, a horse nickers. He looks toward the window and then back at his rag.

EZEKIEL
Wiley, I don't believe I've asked where you're headed.

WILEY
South.

EZEKIEL
South's a big direction.

WILEY
It's the only one I've got.

JOSIAH
We're delivering cattle to Dalton.

WILEY
(sharp)
Josiah.

JOSIAH
Sorry, Wiley.

Silence. The reverend turns a page. The stove ticks.

EZEKIEL
Dalton's a fine town. I knew a man there once. Tall fella. Scar on his lip. Went by Cutter.

Wiley does not move. Josiah's knife hangs in the air over the wood.

EZEKIEL (CONT'D)
Funny thing. He used to come in here and play solitaire. Same as you. Always laid the black eight on the red nine.

Wiley looks down at the table. The black eight lies on the red nine.

WILEY
Coincidence.

EZEKIEL
That's what I told myself.

The reverend closes his Bible. He slips a hand inside his coat. Josiah drops the knife and rises, hands wide. Ezekiel does not stop wiping the counter. He bends, as if reaching for a bottle, and comes up with a double-barreled shotgun pointed at the table.

EZEKIEL (CONT'D)
Gentlemen. There's a marshal in the next room. He's been listening.

REV. MOREHOUSE
(softly)
Mr. Rourke. I am the marshal.

He opens his coat to reveal a star pinned to the lining. Wiley slowly lays down his cards.

WILEY
Well, I'll be.

EZEKIEL
You may be, son. The trial's in Dalton.
